#!/usr/bin/env bash

lord --verbose << "EOF"
I want you to read through ALL of [12345]-*.tex, just the Hebrew and English, and ignoring my comments.

As you're doing that, I want you to write yourself a massive mega-prompt
explaining everything you notice about the style of Friedman's english
translation and the habits it has about how to translate which Hebrew
words, phrases, grammar, etc. into English.

Take note of his habits regarding every single Hebrew word.
Does he always translate it to the same English word?
What word is that? How does he choose to represent the Hebrew
grammar of different authors and sources in English?

I want you to take these notes and write this massive mega-prompt
because I want to eventually use that prompt as notes with the
intention of having you read the remaining books of the Hebrew
bible in Hebrew and translate them all into English *exactly
how you would if you were Friedman.* That English translation
will be the basis of what we do going forward. That's the goal.

For now, don't try to generate english translations of hebrew
books we haven't covered yet. Just read the torah, as found in
the combined tex files in [12345]-*.tex, and take exhaustive
concordance-like notes (in addition to some style and vibes
and personality type notes) on everything you think you'll
eventually need to know in order to simulate the same
incredible translation style on books he never had time
to get around to translating. We're gonna make this guy
immortal, eternal, and Nameless.

Go do that now, and make it perfect.
EOF
